1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,528
When the aortic valve becomes severely
narrowed, it can be hard for the heart to

2
00:00:04,621 --> 00:00:06,420
pump blood out to the body.

3
00:00:07,117 --> 00:00:12,585
Transcatheter aortic valve implantation,
called TAVI or

4
00:00:12,678 --> 00:00:17,020
TAVR, places a new valve inside the old
one.

5
00:00:17,624 --> 00:00:22,721
The aortic valve sits between the heart’s
main pumping chamber and the aorta, the

6
00:00:22,790 --> 00:00:25,600
large artery that carries blood to the
body.

7
00:00:26,296 --> 00:00:30,070
Calcium can stiffen the valve’s flaps so
they open less.

8
00:00:30,766 --> 00:00:36,177
This is aortic stenosis. It can cause
breathlessness, chest discomfort or

9
00:00:36,258 --> 00:00:37,152
fainting.

10
00:00:37,848 --> 00:00:42,957
A heart ultrasound, called an echo, shows
how tightly the valve is narrowed.

11
00:00:43,468 --> 00:00:48,959
A CT scan helps the team plan where a new
valve could fit and how to reach it.

12
00:00:49,563 --> 00:00:54,463
The heart team reviews your symptoms,
anatomy, overall health and preferences

13
00:00:54,521 --> 00:00:59,954
with you. Surgical valve replacement may be
a better option for some people.

14
00:01:00,558 --> 00:01:05,550
In this example, the team reaches the valve
through the femoral artery in the groin.

15
00:01:05,991 --> 00:01:11,401
A guidewire travels through the arteries in
the pelvis, up the aorta and across the

16
00:01:11,494 --> 00:01:14,397
narrowed valve into the heart’s pumping
chamber.

17
00:01:14,907 --> 00:01:17,566
Live X-rays help guide the equipment.

18
00:01:18,007 --> 00:01:21,421
The new valve has tissue flaps held in a
metal frame.

19
00:01:21,931 --> 00:01:27,226
Here, it is compressed around a folded
balloon on the end of a delivery catheter.

20
00:01:27,923 --> 00:01:31,870
The catheter follows the guidewire to the
opening of the old valve.

21
00:01:32,474 --> 00:01:37,478
Once the valve is in position, the balloon
inflates and opens the metal frame.

22
00:01:38,081 --> 00:01:40,485
The frame pushes the old flaps aside.

23
00:01:40,694 --> 00:01:43,655
The team deflates and removes the balloon
and catheter.

24
00:01:43,898 --> 00:01:48,647
The new valve stays in place, and its
tissue flaps now open and close with each

25
00:01:48,693 --> 00:01:49,436
heartbeat.

26
00:01:50,133 --> 00:01:54,859
The team uses heart imaging to check how
well blood flows through the new valve and

27
00:01:54,917 --> 00:01:56,588
whether blood leaks around it.

28
00:01:57,285 --> 00:02:01,929
They also check the heart rhythm and close
the opening in the groin artery when the

29
00:02:01,987 --> 00:02:03,334
equipment comes out.

30
00:02:04,147 --> 00:02:08,245
In recovery, staff check your heart rhythm
and groin site.

31
00:02:08,849 --> 00:02:14,085
Risks include bleeding, injury to a blood
vessel, stroke, and changes in heart

32
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:17,162
rhythm. Some people need a pacemaker.

33
00:02:17,336 --> 00:02:20,343
How long you stay in hospital depends on
your recovery.

34
00:02:20,854 --> 00:02:25,393
Before going home, ask for written
instructions on medicines, wound care,

35
00:02:25,451 --> 00:02:27,669
activity, driving and follow-up.

36
00:02:28,482 --> 00:02:31,198
Mild groin bruising or soreness can happen.

37
00:02:31,709 --> 00:02:36,330
Call your care team if the site becomes
more red or painful, starts draining, or

38
00:02:36,388 --> 00:02:37,723
you develop a fever.

39
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:43,226
Call your local emergency number for heavy
bleeding or bleeding that won’t stop,

40
00:02:43,667 --> 00:02:48,056
severe chest pain, sudden trouble
breathing, or signs of stroke.

41
00:02:48,752 --> 00:02:53,663
These include sudden face drooping, arm
weakness or trouble speaking.

42
00:02:54,778 --> 00:02:59,410
TAVI gives the heart a new valve for blood
to flow through.

43
00:03:00,014 --> 00:03:04,182
Keep your follow-up appointments so the
team can check how it’s working.
